605:
It is hereby determined that:
Discharges to the county's municipal separate storm sewer system (hereinafter referenced as "MS4") that are not composed entirely of stormwater runoff contribute to increased nonpoint source pollution and degradation of receiving waters;
These non-stormwater discharges occur because of spills, dumping and improper connections to the MS4 from residential, industrial, commercial or institutional establishments.
These non-stormwater discharges not only impact waterways individually, but geographically dispersed, small volume non-stormwater discharges can have cumulative impacts on receiving waters.
The impacts of these discharges adversely affect public health and safety, drinking water supplies, recreation, fish and other aquatic life, property values and other uses of lands and waters;
These impacts can be minimized through the regulation of spills, dumping and discharges into the MS4;
Localities in the State of Georgia are required to comply with a number of State and Federal laws, regulations and permits which require a locality to address the impacts of stormwater runoff quality and nonpoint source pollution due to improper non-stormwater discharges to the MS4;
Therefore, the Coweta County Commissioners adopt this ordinance [from which this article derives] to prohibit such non-stormwater discharges to the MS4. It is determined that the regulation of spills, improper dumping and discharges to the MS4 is in the public interest and will prevent threats to public health and safety, and the environment.
(Ord. of 3-1-05; Ord. No. 012-19, 3-26-19; Ord. No. 062-19, 12-17-19)

618:
Water use priorities for potable water are based on the Act and regulation and listed as follows:
(1) Emergency facilities for essential life support measures;
(2) Domestic and personal use, including drinking, cooking, washing, sanitary purposes, and health-related activities;
(3) Farm uses;
(4) Industrial uses (those industries on public water system);
(5) Other uses such as lawn sprinkling, noncommercial car washing, garden watering, etc.;
(6) Outdoor recreational uses.
Under the four levels of drought contingency planning, the following applies as summarized below and in Table 3.1 for potable water use:
Level I: All uses can continue, but with voluntary constraint. The county may institute landscape irrigation restrictions or other demand management strategies. The water consumption reduction goal for Level I is five percent.
Level II: This level would mandate water conservation. Industries would not be curtailed, but would be expected to minimize water consumption. All residential customers will be required to maximize conservation and will be sensitized to the fact that the next step will be severe. The residential water consumption reduction requirement for Level II is ten percent below the consumption in the month prior to imposition of restrictions or the average of the usage in December, January and February, whichever is less. Any consumption above that permitted by this level will be charged the bulk water rate.
Level III: This is the level at which the farm uses will be asked to participate in voluntary water conservation measures. Outdoor watering in residential landscapes will be allowed only once a week by an odd/even day watering system. Commercial and development landscaping will be restricted to outdoor watering once a week as designated by the county. Commercial businesses are expected to implement measures that will reduce their water consumption with a requirement of 25 percent reduction. All industrial users that use proves water are expected to implement conservation measures with a goal of 15 percent less water consumption. Operation of swimming pools and decorative fountains will be limited to use for make-up water. Draining and refilling pools will be prohibited. Water cut-off and fines can be implemented to enforce water conservation requirements. All non-essential water use will be prohibited. The residential water consumption reduction requirement for Level III is 20 percent.
Level IV: At this level, emergency procedures would come into effect, giving priority to potable uses. At this level of drought emergency, all outdoor water use except emergency response and farm uses will be prohibited, mandatory conservation will be in effect, and the county will monitor the situation on a weekly basis. Major industries will be reviewed on a case-by-case basis to evaluate opportunities for optimal water conservation. Violators would be traced through the billing system and observation by county employees, and could face loss of water service. Shutdown of some industries will be mandated as a last resort. The residential water consumption reduction requirement for Level IV is 30 percent.
Enforcement. The county shall reserve the right to terminate water services for repeat violations of emergency water conservation procedures during drought conditions or when it appears that water withdrawal permit limits may be exceeded. Written warnings can be issued for first time violations. A second offense will result in the termination of water service. Service may be restored upon request and payment of a reconnect fee at the water and sewer department office. In the event of a third offense, the violator will be subject to fines or other penalties as dictated by the Coweta County Board of Commissioners that may include action under the Criminal Code of Georgia.
(Ord. of 4-25-00)

