(g) Animal vocalizations and landscaping and construction activities. Instead of the limitations set forth in subsections 4 and 5, the noise limitations set forth in this subsection 7 apply to the following noises audible in residential areas:
(1) Animal vocalizations. See chapter 10 Animals, section 10-92 Animal noise in the Code of Ordinances.
(2) Landscaping. Power tools used for landscaping or yard maintenance which result in noise plainly audible in a sealed dwelling located 100 feet or more away from the property on which the noise originates upon the premises of a proximate outdoor recreational area that is located 300 feet or more from where the noise originates shall be operated only between the hours of 7:00 a.m. and 9:00 p.m.
(3) Construction and demolition activity. Construction and demolition activity or deliveries which result in noise plainly audible in a sealed dwelling located 100 feet or more away from the property on which the noise originates or upon the premises of a proximate outdoor recreational area that is located 300 feet or more from where the noise originates shall only be performed between the hours of 7:00 a.m. and 7:00 p.m. on weekdays, or between the hours of 8:00 a.m. and 5:00 p.m. on weekends.
(h) Temporary or periodic variances.
(1) Any person or entity requesting temporary or periodic relief from this section shall apply for a temporary or periodic variance. The county administrator, or his designee, may issue a temporary or periodic variance pursuant to the following procedures and standards.
(2) Applications for temporary or periodic variances shall state:
a. The nature and location of the noise source for which such application is made;
b. The reason for which a variance is requested, including any unnecessary hardship that will result to the applicant or the public if the variance is not granted;
c. An explanation of how the noise, if a variance is granted, will affect neighboring residents;
d. The times, nature, and intensity of noise that will occur during the period of the variance;
e. A description of the noise control measures to be taken by the applicant to minimize the noise and the impact therefrom; and
f. The name, address, and means of contacting a responsible party who will have control over the noise for which a variance is requested.
(3) Temporary or periodic variances shall be granted or denied based upon consideration of the following criteria:
a. The times and duration of the noise for which a variance is requested;
b. The effect of the noise for which a variance is requested on neighboring residents;
c. Any undue hardship which application of this section will impose on the applicant or the public;
d. The social or economic value of the activity that will generate the noise for which a variance is requested; and
e. Whether there is a reasonable alternative to the applicant.
(4) A temporary or periodic variance must be in writing. The county administrator, or his designee, may not verbally grant a temporary or periodic variance.
(5) Each temporary or periodic variance shall specifically state the time period for which the variance is applicable and shall be effective only for the time period stated in the variance.
(6) Temporary or periodic variances may be granted subject to conditions imposed by the county administrator, or his designee, for the purposes of minimizing the effect of the noise permitted by the variance.
(7) A temporary or periodic variance may be revoked by the county administrator, or his designee, and the issuance of future temporary or periodic variances withheld, if there is a:
a. Violation of one or more conditions of the variance;
b. Material misrepresentation of fact in the variance application; or
c. Material change in any of the circumstances relied upon by the county administrator, or his designee, in granting the variance.
(i) Penalties for violations. Violation of this section shall be punishable as provided in chapter 1 General provisions, section 1-15 of the Code of Ordinances.
